{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And Pharaoh said, etc., saying, etc.</b> One needs to know the intent of his saying <b>lemor</b> (saying). It is also difficult that he says <b>your father</b>, etc., <b>have come to you</b> — behold, this comes to teach, and yet it turns out to learn from the very thing itself that it teaches." "<b>However</b>, you will find that when Pharaoh spoke to Joseph concerning the matter of his brothers — that they should take their father and come — he said (Genesis 45:17): ״אֱמוֹר אֶל אַחֶיךָ זֹאת עֲשׂוּ וְגוֹ׳ וּקְחוּ אֶת אֲבִיכֶם וְגוֹ׳ וּבֹאוּ אֵלָי״. Now this matter carries two implications: the first, that he should speak to them in Pharaoh's name, for Pharaoh is the one commanding; and the second, that Joseph should say so to them on his own authority. And this second reading is more fitting, for we find that he said afterward (Genesis 45:19): ״וְאַתָּה צֻוֵּיתָה״, etc. — and if all of it were Pharaoh's words, there would be no need to say that he took permission from Pharaoh, since it is all Pharaoh's words. Now you will find that when Joseph spoke to his brothers before speaking to Pharaoh, he said to them (Genesis 46:31): ״אֶעֱלֶה וְאַגִּידָה לְפַרְעֹה וְגוֹ׳ אַחַי וְגוֹ׳ בָּאוּ אֵלַי״ — thereby he revealed his intention that he himself is the one commanding them to come to him, and behold, the men came to him, and this validates the second reading. And when he spoke to Pharaoh he was not precise to say ״they came to me,״ but simply ״my brothers, etc., have come from the land of Canaan,״ etc. This indicates that their coming was for Pharaoh, and therefore he informs him, saying: they fulfilled your words — as I explained in the verse adjacent to this one. Therefore Pharaoh answered Joseph, saying: <b>your father and your brothers have come to you</b> — meaning, so that you may say that in the coming of your brothers they have come to you. For my intent in saying ״Speak to your brothers: do this״ is not that you should speak to them as my emissary, as though I am the one commanding, but rather I have authorized you to speak to them in your own name that they should come to you. And I have already authorized you to give them the good of the land of Egypt, and if so, ״the land of Egypt is before you, in the best of the land,״ etc." "And the difference between whether the matter would be by Pharaoh's command or by Joseph's authority involves both a disparaging aspect and a praiseworthy aspect. The disparaging aspect: that he removed the obligation from himself toward them; and further, because it is the custom of the kingdom that when a king grants greatness or benefit to a single person, it is for him an eternal gift. Therefore, if the words spoken were from Pharaoh's mouth, behold they come by royal decree, and there is an obligation upon the king to bear their yoke permanently, and the best of the land would be given to them as an eternal holding in his kingdom. And therefore Pharaoh was careful that the matter should not come from himself but from Joseph, so that the obligation would be upon him and not upon Pharaoh — and in this his gift is not eternal, for Joseph is only a governor in the land of Egypt and has no royal title, and his gift would endure only as long as the king desires it. And through this he further intended that Joseph should not think that Pharaoh needs them, and that for the sake of the kingdom he sent for them to be mighty warriors, to serve as an army and a bulwark for his kingdom. Therefore he said to him that Pharaoh himself has no need for them." "<b>And</b> the praiseworthy aspect is this: why should Pharaoh need to inform him of the matter as a man who has no power in his hand to do anything until he takes permission from the king? Has permission not already been given to him over everything — whatever he does, he does? And this is what he means by <b>they have come to you</b> — you are worthy that they should come for your sake, and by the authority of your word ״the land of Egypt is before you, in the best of the land,״ etc., without any need to take permission from me. And along this line the word ״lemor״ (saying) may further be explained: that he gave him permission to say the things spoken to him, following what they say in chapter 1 of tractate Yoma (4a), and this is its language: Rabbi Manasya said: From where do we learn that one who wishes to say something to his fellow may not say it until the other tells him ״speak״? For it is written: ״וַיְדַבֵּר ה׳ וְגוֹ׳ לֵאמֹר״ — end of quotation. And therefore Pharaoh needed to grant him permission, for every honor and greatness said to Joseph came from Pharaoh's mouth. And even though some diminution of Pharaoh's stature might result through the mediation of these words, it is of no consequence." "Further, the word <b>elekha</b> (to you) may allude to telling him that the reason he commanded them to come is only for what touches upon Joseph's honor — so that his family would be recognized in Egypt and he would not be regarded as coming from lowly people. And although his brothers were recognized on the day he made himself known to them, he is still under suspicion: perhaps he saw a band of young men and called himself by their name, as stated in the words of the Sages of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 100:9) on the verse (Genesis 50:21) ״וַיְנַחֵם אוֹתָם וַיְדַבֵּר עַל לִבָּם״. And with the coming of his father and his brothers the matter will be believed. And this is what he means by <b>they have come to you</b> — for your need and for your honor is their coming."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:49:38.800487078Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}